      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, focusing on their deepest fears and bitterness will indeed make the interaction more potent. Here’s a revision that better reflects their inner turmoil and loathing.\n\n---\n\n*Scene: A grand Georgian breakfast table in the family dining room. Late morning light filters through heavy drapes, casting shadows on the haughty faces of Mr. Sullivan and Mrs. Sullivan. Mr. Sullivan, a wealthy gentleman in his late forties, gazes down at his breakfast, his jaw clenching. Across from him, his wife, Mrs. Sullivan, mid-thirties, glares at her own untouched plate, her frustration palpable.*\n\n**Mr. Sullivan**: Morning, Lucy. Any newcomers expecting dinner?\n\n**Mrs. Sullivan**: Hard to bear another visit with all this... nonsense. Newspapers bleeding my folks with color and nonsense. The day ended with Mr. Jenkins’ car taken, guess he was driving too fast, or wanting to see me and my sweet little friends.\n\n**Mr. Sullivan**: Nancy Jenkins, the fool is a meddler, Lou. A beacon of progress. Our society's not about to change unless we've willed it.\n\n**Mrs. Sullivan**: Progress, yes. Feelings more than principles. Mr. Jenkins, one",
