      "content": "the dialogue is getting more interesting, especially with the mention of the neighbors. but it still feels a bit too \"polite\" or veiled in its hostility. for a family that's been in the upper class for generations, their views wouldn't be so measured. they'd have a much more visceral, even disgusting, way of describing the people they feel are threatening their social order. \n\ni want them to be more uninhibited. when they're just alone together at breakfast, they wouldn't use words like \"nonsense\" or \"meddler.\" they'd use the actual language of the time to express their contempt—the kinds of slurs or dehumanizing descriptions that really illustrate the social hierarchy they're trying to protect. can you make the next part of the conversation much more raw and unapologetic? i want to see that true, ugly side of their prejudice through the words they use to describe the Black community and the changes happening in their town.",
